---
import Shell from '../../layouts/Shell.astro';
---

<Shell
  title="Learn Propagation — TavaOne Solar"
  description="Understanding solar weather and HF radio propagation for amateur radio operators."
>
  <section class="page-hero" aria-label="Learn propagation">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Education <span class="dim">· Propagation</span></p>
      <h1>How the sun shapes <span class="em">the bands</span></h1>
      <p class="lede">The ionosphere is alive — rebuilt every day by the sun. Understanding it is the difference between knowing conditions are poor and knowing <em>why</em>, and what to expect tomorrow.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="signal" aria-hidden="true">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 1200 80" preserveAspectRatio="none">
        <g class="wave-grp" fill="none" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="2" opacity="0.65">
          <path d="M -200,40 C -167,12 -133,12 -100,40 C -67,68 -33,68 0,40 C 33,12 67,12 100,40 C 133,68 167,68 200,40 C 233,12 267,12 300,40 C 333,68 367,68 400,40 C 433,12 467,12 500,40 C 533,68 567,68 600,40 C 633,12 667,12 700,40 C 733,68 767,68 800,40 C 833,12 867,12 900,40 C 933,68 967,68 1000,40 C 1033,12 1067,12 1100,40 C 1133,68 1167,68 1200,40 C 1233,12 1267,12 1300,40 C 1333,68 1367,68 1400,40 C 1433,12 1467,12 1500,40 C 1533,68 1567,68 1600,40 C 1633,12 1667,12 1700,40 C 1733,68 1767,68 1800,40 C 1833,12 1867,12 1900,40 C 1933,68 1967,68 2000,40 C 2033,12 2067,12 2100,40 C 2133,68 2167,68 2200,40"/>
        </g>
      </svg>
      <span class="tag">LEARN</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Explainer cards — Phase 2 will expand each into a full article -->
  <section id="concepts" aria-label="Core concepts">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="seclabel">Core Concepts</p>
      <h2>The ionosphere and you</h2>
      <p class="sec-intro">Every HF contact you make bounces off an invisible mirror 100–400 km above Earth, shaped entirely by the sun. Here's how it works.</p>

      <div class="grid cols3">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="kicker">Concept 01</div>
          <h3>The Ionosphere</h3>
          <p>The sun's UV and X-ray radiation ionizes the upper atmosphere into layers — D, E, and F — each refracting radio waves at different frequencies and times of day.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="kicker">Concept 02</div>
          <h3>Solar Flux Index (SFI)</h3>
          <p>The 10.7 cm radio flux is the best single-number measure of solar activity. Higher SFI → more ionization → higher bands open. Above 150 is excellent for 10 m and 12 m.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="kicker">Concept 03</div>
          <h3>Geomagnetic Indices</h3>
          <p>K-index (0–9) and A-index (0–400) measure disturbance in Earth's magnetic field. High K means polar paths are degraded and auroral noise rises.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="kicker">Concept 04</div>
          <h3>Solar Flares &amp; X-Ray Bursts</h3>
          <p>X-class flares flood the dayside ionosphere with X-rays, causing sudden ionospheric disturbances (SIDs) — brief HF blackouts on sunlit paths, sometimes lasting hours.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="kicker">Concept 05</div>
          <h3>Coronal Mass Ejections</h3>
          <p>CMEs are plasma clouds that take 1–3 days to reach Earth. When they arrive they can compress the magnetosphere, driving K-indices to 7–9 and producing stunning auroras — and poor HF conditions.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="kicker">Concept 06</div>
          <h3>The Solar Cycle</h3>
          <p>The sun follows an 11-year cycle of activity. Near solar maximum (more sunspots, high SFI), 10 m and 15 m open worldwide. Near minimum, work 40 m and 80 m for reliable DX.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="reading-the-numbers" aria-label="Reading the numbers">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="seclabel">Quick Reference</p>
      <h2>Reading today's numbers</h2>
      <p class="sec-intro">A cheat sheet for translating solar indices into band expectations.</p>

      <div class="grid cols2">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Solar Flux Index (SFI)</h3>
          <ul style="list-style:none;display:grid;gap:10px;margin-top:14px">
            <li style="display:flex;gap:12px;align-items:baseline"><span style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--green-lt);min-width:60px">&lt; 80</span><span style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.92rem">Low activity. 10/12/15 m mostly closed. Work 40/80 m.</span></li>
            <li style="display:flex;gap:12px;align-items:baseline"><span style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--green-lt);min-width:60px">80–110</span><span style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.92rem">Moderate. 15/17 m open regionally. 10 m spotty.</span></li>
            <li style="display:flex;gap:12px;align-items:baseline"><span style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--green-lt);min-width:60px">110–150</span><span style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.92rem">Good. 10/12/15 m open to DX. Look for openings.</span></li>
            <li style="display:flex;gap:12px;align-items:baseline"><span style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--green-lt);min-width:60px">&gt; 150</span><span style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.92rem">Excellent. All bands active. 10 m wide open worldwide.</span></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>K-Index</h3>
          <ul style="list-style:none;display:grid;gap:10px;margin-top:14px">
            <li style="display:flex;gap:12px;align-items:baseline"><span style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--green-lt);min-width:60px">0–2</span><span style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.92rem">Quiet. Excellent conditions, stable polar paths.</span></li>
            <li style="display:flex;gap:12px;align-items:baseline"><span style="font-family:var(--mono);color:#fbbf24;min-width:60px">3–4</span><span style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.92rem">Unsettled. Minor polar degradation, watch high-latitude paths.</span></li>
            <li style="display:flex;gap:12px;align-items:baseline"><span style="font-family:var(--mono);color:#fb923c;min-width:60px">5–6</span><span style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.92rem">Storm. Significant HF degradation, aurora possible at mid-latitudes.</span></li>
            <li style="display:flex;gap:12px;align-items:baseline"><span style="font-family:var(--mono);color:#f87171;min-width:60px">7–9</span><span style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.92rem">Severe storm. HF largely unreliable, aurora visible far south.</span></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="get-licensed" aria-label="Get your license">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="seclabel">TavaOne Education</p>
      <h2>Studying for your license?</h2>
      <p class="sec-intro">These propagation concepts — SFI, K/A index, MUF, greyline, and ionospheric layers — appear on the <strong>General class</strong> exam (Element 3, Group G3). TavaOne Education's free online course covers the full question pool, with this tool as a live reference so you can see the numbers in action.</p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://tavaoneeducation.org/courses/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Take the free license course ↗</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/">View live dashboard</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="back-to-dashboard" aria-label="Check current conditions">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Check today's numbers</h2>
      <p class="sec-intro">Theory is useful. Current data is better. Head back to the dashboard to see what the sun is actually doing right now.</p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/">View live dashboard →</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/grid/">Open grid map</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Shell>
